151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nSuppose that $f$ is a polynomial such that \\[(x^2-1)\\cdot f(x)=5x^6-x^5+3x^4+x^3 - 25x^2 +38x -17.\\]What is the degree of $f$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"026a923182ed296f7f3a46406bf89b2578482a4db275abfa41bf4851989f0613"},"seconds":9.44178819656372,"seed":315041721621290121,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the degree of the polynomial \\( f(x) \\), we start by analyzing the given equation:\n\n\\[\n(x^2-1) \\cdot f(x) = 5x^6 - x^5 + 3x^4 + x^3 - 25x^2 + 38x - 17.\n\\]\n\nFirst, we identify the degree of the polynomial on the right-hand side. The highest power of \\( x \\) in the polynomial \\( 5x^6 - x^5 + 3x^4 + x^3 - 25x^2 + 38x - 17 \\) is \\( x^6 \\). Therefore, the degree of the polynomial on the right-hand side is 6.\n\nNext, we consider the left-hand side of the equation, which is \\((x^2-1) \\cdot f(x)\\). The polynomial \\( x^2 - 1 \\) has a degree of 2. Let the degree of \\( f(x) \\) be \\( n \\). Then, the degree of the product \\((x^2-1) \\cdot f(x)\\) is the sum of the degrees of \\( x^2 - 1 \\) and \\( f(x) \\):\n\n\\[\n\\deg((x^2-1) \\cdot f(x)) = \\deg(x^2-1) + \\deg(f(x)) = 2 + n.\n\\]\n\nSince the degrees of the polynomials on both sides of the equation must be equal, we set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n2 + n = 6.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\nn = 6 - 2 = 4.\n\\]\n\nThus, the degree of \\( f(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:656"}
